{"records":[{"id":"fm4h3mphcr","title":"NGS, confocal microscopy and flow cytometry data of CRISPR/Cas9-edited ARE1 and KAT2 genes in transgenic maize in vitro tissues exposed to chromatin modifiers","description":"This dataset contains raw NGS reads (NGS_data folder), fluorescence microscopy photos (Fig7B Microscopy Originals.lif) and flow cytometry data (@Fig6 FACS original data.zip) derived from transgenic maize (Zea mays L.) in vitro tissues exposed to chromatin modifiers. They are presented in research article ˝Variable Alterations in Frequencies of CRISPR/Cas9-Induced Nucleotide Deletion and Insertion in Transgenic Maize Callus Tissues Exposed to Chromatin Modifiers˝.\nStable transgenic maize lines harboring active CRISPR/Cas9 vector systems targeting either the abnormal cytokinin response 1 (ARE1) gene or the potassium channel gene (KAT2) were used. Mutagenic events following exposure to elevated temperatures (37 °C for 48 h) and various chromatin-modifying compounds were quantified. As a novel approach, the mutagenic potential of combining chromatin-modifying agents with heat exposure was examined.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-24T23:17:12.229Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fm4h3mphcr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fm4h3mphcr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Bettina","last_name":"Nagy","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Györgyi","last_name":"Ferenc","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ferhan","last_name":"Ayaydin","orcid":"0000-0003-4450-0667"},{"first_name":"Divya","last_name":"Teja","orcid":"0009-0001-0210-7878"},{"first_name":"Dénes","last_name":"Dudits","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zoltán","last_name":"Zombori","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nikolett","last_name":"Laszlo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aysha","last_name":"Jameel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gábor","last_name":"Horváth","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"EU’s Horizon 2020 Research and Innovation Program","grant_id":" 739593"},{"id":"https://ror.org/03g2am276","name":"National Research, Development and Innovation Office","grant_id":"RRF 2.3.1–21-2022- 00007 grant for the ‘National Laboratory Program of Agrobiotechnology and Precision Plant Breeding to Support Food Safety’"}],"categories":["Confocal Microscopy","Flow Cytometry","Next Generation Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wph373m86f","title":"RNA-seq (FPKM), RT-qPCR, and qPCR datasets of host responses in Orientia tsutsugamushi-infected mouse macrophages","description":"This dataset contains gene expression and validation data derived from primary mouse macrophages infected with Orientia tsutsugamushi. The primary dataset consists of a genome-wide RNA-seq expression matrix reported as FPKM (Fragments Per Kilobase of transcript per Million mapped reads) values for 28,000+ genes, aligned to the Mus musculus reference genome (mm10). The matrix includes genomic annotations (gene ID, chromosome, genomic coordinates) and normalized expression values across multiple experimental conditions and biological replicates.\nIn addition to the RNA-seq dataset, the file includes qRT-PCR validation data for selected host genes (Figures 1, 4, and 5), with expression levels normalized to Gapdh. The dataset also contains bacterial burden measurements quantified by qPCR targeting the 47-kDa gene, expressed as gene copy number per sample.\nTogether, these data provide both transcriptome-wide expression profiles (FPKM) and targeted validation of host response and bacterial load under different infection conditions.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-20T15:01:42.653Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wph373m86f","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wph373m86f"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dario","last_name":"Villacreses","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"AI193808"},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"AI132674"},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"AI007526"},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"AI060549"},{"id":"https://ror.org/016tfm930","name":"The University of Texas Medical Branch at Galveston","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Immunology","RNA Sequencing","Host Defense","Innate Immunity"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hh46ymyxkk","title":"CRISPR amplicon sequencing of human TAP1 gene of CRISPR/Cas9-edited mono-allelic HLA class I-expressing cell lines","description":"TAP-deficient monoallelic HLA class I-expressing cell lines were generated by sequential transduction of the HLA-null human B cell line 721.221 with lentiviral expression constructs encoding (i) Cas9 protein linked to blasticidin resistance gene via a self-cleaving P2A peptide, (ii) 18 distinct HLA class I alleles that provided >99% global coverage (A*0101, A*0201, A*0301, A*2402, B*0702, B*0801, B*1402, B*1501, B*2705, B*3501, B*3901, B*4001, B*4402, B*5201, B*5701, B*5801, B*8101 and Cw*0701) under puromycin resistance linked via an internal ribosome entry sequence (IRES), and (iii) a single guide RNA (sgRNA) directed towards human TAP1 under neomycin/G418 resistance linked via an IRES. Successful CRISPR/Cas9 editing of the TAP1 gene was confirmed by amplicon sequencing of genomic DNA for each mono-allelic cell line. Amplicon sequencing was carried out by the MGH DNA Sequencing Core Facility. \n\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-29T06:17:42.059Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hh46ymyxkk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hh46ymyxkk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Anusha","last_name":"Nathan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Vaccine","Human Immunodeficiency Virus","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wkd5wp9zwk","title":"data for CasPER, a method for directed evolution in genomic contexts using mutagenesis and CRISPR/Cas9","description":"This is the raw NGS sequencing data as python analysis for the paper thats currently titled:\nCasPER, a method for directed evolution in genomic contexts using mutagenesis and CRISPR/Cas9","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-06-14T11:58:27.336Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wkd5wp9zwk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wkd5wp9zwk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lasse Ebdrup","last_name":"Pedersen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Yeast","Data Analysis","Next Generation Sequencing"],"journal_issn":["10967176"]},{"id":"nknd6w2kbm","title":"Supplementary information's for accepted manuscript in Cell press \"Star protocols\" :A Simple Method to Isolate a Single Human Cell PRDX1 Knockout Generated by CRISPR-Cas9 System","description":"Revised Supplementary information's for accepted manuscript in Cell press \"Star protocols\" :A Simple Method to Isolate a Single Human Cell PRDX1 Knockout Generated by CRISPR-Cas9 System","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-02-09T15:20:06.778Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nknd6w2kbm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nknd6w2kbm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mustapha","last_name":"Aouida","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Life Sciences","Cell Biology","Genetic Engineering","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y4d9rzrnr6","title":"Specific macrophage RhoA targeting (SMART) CRISPR-Cas9 for mitigating osteoclastogenesis-induced joint damage in inflammatory arthritis","description":"This is a raw data and supplemental information of the manuscript \"Specific macrophage RhoA targeting (SMART) CRISPR-Cas9 for mitigating osteoclastogenesis-induced joint damage in inflammatory arthritis\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-14T19:58:59.452Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y4d9rzrnr6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y4d9rzrnr6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"JINGQIN","last_name":"CHEN","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Rheumatoid Arthritis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zjtbfxhkg3","title":"Raw images of western blots, comets and outline of CRISPR-CAS9 targets and sequence disruption","description":"Sheet 1-6 displays raw western blot images, Sheet 7 displays full images of BrdU comet assay, and Sheet 8 outlines  CRISPR-CAS9 targeting and sequencing ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-07-31T17:32:36.35Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zjtbfxhkg3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zjtbfxhkg3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"sharon","last_name":"cantor","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","DNA Replication","Fanconi Anemia"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7g2pbbrn4m","title":"Targetd inactivation of oncogenic drivers in tumor cells derived from adult muscle stem cells","description":"Representative movies of early-passaged tumor propagating cells (TPC) FACS-purified from primary murine tumors. These cells are lineage-traced through activation of a Rosa26-Tomato reporter allele.\n\n\"TPC Duxbl\" contain copy number amplification of the duxbl gene identified through genomic sequencing.\n\"TPC Yap1\" contain copy number amplification of the yap1 gene identified through genomic sequencing.\n\nThese cells were individually subjected to lentiviruses encoding shrnas to inactivate either duxbl or yap1 or to non-coding lentivurs (plko.1 control).\n\nNote that inactivation of duxbl only affected growth of TPC Duxbl but not TPC Yap1. Conversely inactivation of yap1 only affected growth of TPC Yap1l but not TPC Duxbl.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-10-23T20:10:51.209Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7g2pbbrn4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7g2pbbrn4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Johnny","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Stem Cell","Regenerative Medicine","Muscle"],"journal_issn":["19345909"]},{"id":"sxrvzkr4hh","title":"Pooled in vitro and in vivo CRISPR-Cas9 screening identifies tumor suppressors in human colon organoids","description":"UMI validation screen, original data from in vivo tumor suppressor CRISPR/Cas9 screening experiment. Figure 6.\nExperimental setup: 281 tumor suppressor gRNAs, positive controls, neutral controls and gRNAs targeting essential genes were introduced into the CRISPR-UMI lentivirus (Michlits et al., 2017). Transduced AK organoids (APC-KO and KRASG12D) were transplanted subcutaneously in NSG mice followed by barcode sequencing of the tumor pool (from 10 mice) and 5 individual tumors. Clone and read number were determined by next generation sequencing. The conventional gRNA enrichment analysis was compared to UMI-CRISPR-screening with outlier removal.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-04-20T15:46:17.868Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sxrvzkr4hh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sxrvzkr4hh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Henner","last_name":"Farin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":105396,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":136,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":105396,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":105074,"restricted":56,"embargoed":266},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1712},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":563},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":396},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":367},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":363},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":350},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":306},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":284},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":201},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":189}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":102960},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1820},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":171},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":100},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":64},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":62},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":46},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":30},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":30},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":447},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":393},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":353},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":265},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":263}]}}